{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790275715,"nonce":"ZnZz9ZMg/g+zWujvnPRAi1sKliY=","title":"Python: an exception leaving a compiled tuple-returning function segfaults: the return copies from a null placeholder","message":"Reproducer (Mac arm64, main 8844ca79 release zypy, no LLVM tier, deterministic; also with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1):\n\n    def f(t: tuple[int, int, int], k: int) -\u003e tuple[int, int, int]:\n        a, b, c = t\n        return (a // k, b, c)\n\n    def main() -\u003e None:\n        n = 0\n        for i in range(3000):\n            try:\n                r = f((i, 2, 3), i % 3)\n                n += r[0]\n            except ZeroDivisionError:\n                n += 1000\n        print(n)\n\n    main()\n\nCPython prints 3249500; zypy exits 139 with nothing printed. One call from module level (interpreted) prints \"caught\".\n\nf returns a 3-tuple, a multi-field struct, so a compiled f returns it through the caller's destination (abi::destination_return_type). The pending-exception exit of its body returns a placeholder built as `bitcast i64 0` to the struct type (seen in fn-step-tier1.hir under ZYNTAX_DUMP_HIR_DIR for a similar function):\n\n    block9:\n        %v60: { i64, i64, i64 } = bitcast %v1   ; %v1 = const 0\n        return %v60\n\nCranelift holds a struct by address, so that placeholder is address 0, and the Return lowering copies the destination's bytes from it (emit_inline_aggregate_copy / memcpy), which faults. The LLVM entry (destination returns landed with the C-llvm-aggregate-entry-abi package) does the same with a memmove from null, so both tiers need the same answer: either the lowering skips the copy on the exception exit (the caller reads the pending exception, never the destination), or the frontend returns a zeroed aggregate rather than a null address.","files":null}]}